From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nate Isern <nate@oxy.so>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Configure thiserror per epoch, and let gnrt vendor keep it

Chromium vendors thiserror 2.x. Astro's `adblock` dependency pulls in 1.x
alongside it, and the two epochs disagree about the one thing
`gnrt_config.toml` states for them:

    [crate.thiserror]
    build_script_outputs = [ "private.rs" ]

2.0.18's build script does `fs::write(out_dir.join("private.rs"), module)`
and `src/lib.rs:291` reads it back with
`include!(concat!(env!("OUT_DIR"), "/private.rs"))`. 1.0.69's build script
writes no file at all -- it only probes the compiler and prints `--cfg`
lines -- and its `src/lib.rs` contains no `include!`. Because the key is
declared under the unversioned crate name, gnrt writes it into BOTH generated
targets, and the 1.x build script action dies going to collect an output that
was never produced:

    FAILED: gen/third_party/rust/thiserror/v1/lib/cargo_flags.rs gen/third_party/rust/thiserror/v1/lib/private.rs
    Traceback (most recent call last):
      File ".../build/rust/gni_impl/run_build_script.py", line 193, in main
        with open(in_path, 'rb') as input:
    FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: '/tmp/tmpditv_9fq/private.rs'

There is no unversioned value that is right. Removing the key breaks 2.x,
which every other Chromium consumer of thiserror uses, and would rewrite a
TRACKED upstream file (`third_party/rust/thiserror/v2/BUILD.gn`) to say
something false. Keeping it breaks 1.x. `remove_build_rs` has the same
problem in the other direction: 1.x tolerates losing its build script, 2.x
does not.

THE MECHANISM UPSTREAM ALREADY DOCUMENTS, AND WHY IT DOES NOT WORK

`gnrt_config.toml`'s own header describes the answer:

    # For crates from multiple epochs that need different configuration for
    # different epochs, use epoch-specific config sections like
    # `[crate."foo@v1"]` or `[crate."foo@v2"]`.  A crate must use either
    # unversioned config (`[crate.foo]`) OR one or more versioned configs
    # (`[crate."foo@v1"]`), but not both.

`BuildConfig::get_crate_config` implements the lookup and
`BuildConfig::validate` enforces the exclusivity. Both are correct. The
mechanism is nevertheless unusable for any crate in the dependency graph,
because `fill_allow_unsafe_settings` (tools/crates/gnrt/vendor.rs) writes its
auto-filled `allow_unsafe` under the bare package name and nothing else:

    let crate_key = &package.package_name;
    let crate_table = get_or_insert_table(top_table, crate_key, new_table_fn);

`get_or_insert_table` inserts. So on the next `gnrt vendor`, a config holding
`[crate."thiserror@v1"]` gains a `[crate.thiserror]` beside it, and the run
after that fails at load with

    Crate 'thiserror' has both unversioned ([crate.thiserror]) and versioned
    ([crate."thiserror@vX"]) config entries. Use one or the other, not both.

That is not a hypothetical: the write happens on every fresh tree, because
the auto-fill runs for every vendored crate. Upstream marks the gap with a
TODO on the same lines, crbug.com/419104870.

WHAT THIS PATCH CHANGES IN GNRT

The loop consults the epoch-scoped key first and falls back to the bare name,
which is the same precedence `get_crate_config` already uses for reading. Two
properties keep the change inert:

  * It only looks at tables that ALREADY exist. A crate with no versioned
    config is written exactly where it was written before -- and after this
    patch, thiserror is the only crate in the file with one, so no other
    crate's behaviour moves at all.
  * It never creates the second table for a crate that has the first, so the
    file it writes still loads.

It is the narrow half of the TODO: enough to stop the writer invalidating a
supported config, not a rework of how epochs are keyed.

WHY NOT SIDESTEP IT

Two routes avoid touching gnrt and both are worse. Dropping thiserror 1.x
means changing what `adblock ^0.9` resolves to, which is a dependency
decision (browser.lock.json, `third_party.adblock_rust`, issue #5) and not a
build-configuration one. Editing the vendored 1.0.69 crate to emit an empty
`private.rs` puts a fabricated file into a third-party tree that the next
`gnrt vendor` overwrites without comment.

MEASURED

Patch applied, `tools/vendor-adblock-rust.sh` re-run:

  * `gnrt_config.toml` came back byte-identical to what it was before the run,
    with no `[crate.thiserror]` beside the two versioned tables.
  * `third_party/rust/thiserror/v1/BUILD.gn` has `build_root` and
    `build_sources` and no `build_script_outputs`.
  * `third_party/rust/thiserror/v2/BUILD.gn` still declares
    `build_script_outputs = [ "private.rs" ]`, unchanged from what upstream
    tracks.

That byte-identical result is on its own WORTHLESS as evidence, and saying so
matters more than the result does. `fill_allow_unsafe_settings` sets
`did_make_edits` only when it INSERTS a key, and on an already-vendored tree
every key is present, so it writes nothing at all -- an unpatched gnrt produces
the same byte-identical file. The check cannot tell a working epoch lookup from
a writer that never ran.

So the branch was forced. `extra_kv` was DELETED from `[crate."thiserror@v1"]`
and the vendoring re-run:

    [crate."thiserror@v1"]
    extra_kv = { allow_unsafe = false }     <- restored, in the versioned table

    $ grep -c '^\[crate\.thiserror\]' gnrt_config.toml
    0

The value came back inside the epoch-scoped table and no unversioned table was
created beside it. That is the branch this patch adds, exercised: without it the
write lands in `[crate.thiserror]` and the NEXT load of the file fails
`BuildConfig::validate` outright. The tree was then restored and re-verified
byte-identical, generated `BUILD.gn` files included.

Then from chromium/src with the committed gn_args/linux.gn, all three of 064,
065 and 066 applied:

    $ ./buildtools/linux64/gn gen out/GateCheck
    Done. Made 29957 targets from 4411 files in 9591ms

    $ ./third_party/ninja/ninja -C out/GateCheck -k 0 \
          obj/third_party/rust/thiserror/v1/lib/libthiserror_lib.rlib \
          obj/third_party/rust/thiserror/v2/lib/libthiserror_lib.rlib
    [1/17] RUST obj/third_party/rust/thiserror/v1/lib/libthiserror_lib.rlib
    [2/17] RUST obj/third_party/rust/thiserror/v2/lib/libthiserror_lib.rlib

`thiserror_lib_v1_build_script_output` runs to completion and both epochs link
-- from DELETED outputs, not from ninja finding nothing to do. The target count
is the vacuity floor: a failed `gn gen` writes no build graph, and every check
downstream of one reads clean against nothing.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX

`//chrome/browser/oxy/adblock/rs:adblock_engine_ffi` still does not link, for a
reason no patch in this wave touches: rmp-serde 0.15.5 against rmp 0.8.15. It is
characterised in 066, the patch that declares rmp.

diff --git a/third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/gnrt_config.toml b/third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/gnrt_config.toml
--- a/third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/gnrt_config.toml
+++ b/third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/gnrt_config.toml
@@ -687,11 +687,19 @@ allow_unsafe = false
 [crate.termcolor.extra_kv]
 allow_unsafe = false
 
-[crate.thiserror]
-build_script_outputs = [ "private.rs" ]
+[crate.thiserror-impl]
 extra_kv = { allow_unsafe = false }
 
-[crate.thiserror-impl]
+# Astro: `build_script_outputs` is not the same for the two vendored epochs, so
+# thiserror cannot be configured under one unversioned key. 2.x's build script
+# writes `$OUT_DIR/private.rs` and `src/lib.rs` include!s it; 1.x's writes no
+# file at all, and declaring one there makes `run_build_script.py` fail with
+# `FileNotFoundError` when it goes to copy the output out of the temp dir.
+[crate."thiserror@v1"]
+extra_kv = { allow_unsafe = false }
+
+[crate."thiserror@v2"]
+build_script_outputs = [ "private.rs" ]
 extra_kv = { allow_unsafe = false }
 
 [crate.timezone_provider.extra_kv]
diff --git a/tools/crates/gnrt/vendor.rs b/tools/crates/gnrt/vendor.rs
--- a/tools/crates/gnrt/vendor.rs
+++ b/tools/crates/gnrt/vendor.rs
@@ -326,10 +326,19 @@ fn fill_allow_unsafe_settings(
     let new_inline_table_fn = || Item::from(InlineTable::new());
     let top_table = get_or_insert_table(&mut doc as &mut Table, "crate", new_table_fn);
     for package in deps.into_iter() {
-        // TODO(https://crbug.com/419104870): In the future an epoch-based `crate_key`
-        // may need to be consulted (in addition-to, or instead-of the `crate_key`
-        // below).
-        let crate_key = &package.package_name;
+        // Write into the epoch-scoped table when the config already has one for
+        // this crate, and only fall back to the unversioned name otherwise.
+        //
+        // `BuildConfig::get_crate_config` reads `<name>@<epoch>` in preference
+        // to `<name>`, and `BuildConfig::validate` rejects a file that carries
+        // both for one crate. So creating the unversioned table here does not
+        // merely add a redundant entry: it makes the next load of the file fail
+        // outright, which is what makes a per-epoch config unusable for any
+        // crate this loop touches. See https://crbug.com/419104870.
+        let epoch_key =
+            format!("{}@{}", package.package_name, Epoch::from_version(&package.version));
+        let crate_key =
+            if top_table.get(&epoch_key).is_some() { &epoch_key } else { &package.package_name };
         let crate_table = get_or_insert_table(top_table, crate_key, new_table_fn);
         let extra_kv_table = get_or_insert_table(crate_table, "extra_kv", new_inline_table_fn);
 
